--- a/docs/prewarm.md
+++ b/docs/prewarm.md
@@ -6,8 +6,9 @@ an empty default KVM VM without guest memory, devices or guest execution.
 A subsequent ordinary restore consumes it. Confidential VM types use the
 ordinary constructor instead. Other hypervisor backends reject preparation.
 
-Preparation is single-use. A repeated request is rejected. Delete discards
-unused preparation; after a failed preparation, replace the VMM process.
+Preparation is single-use. Identical requests are idempotent. Generic prewarm can
+upgrade to one prewarm profile; a nonempty profile cannot be rebound. Delete discards
+unused preparation; after an allocation failure, replace the VMM process.
 These interfaces are proposed extensions, not upstream compatibility promises.
 
 Preparation also compiles the existing virtio seccomp filters before restore.
@@ -21,7 +22,7 @@ initialization stays in the existing CPU restore path. Unused descriptors
 are owned by the prepared VM and dropped with it. Empty IDs with no device profile retain generic prewarm only.
 
 Prepared vCPU IDs must match the actual restored boot CPU count and topology.
-A mismatch is rejected before restoring state or preparing template memory;
+A mismatch is rejected before restoring state;
 unused reserved kernel vCPUs are never silently attached to a different VM.
 
 ## Profile prewarm device workers
@@ -65,3 +66,20 @@ reserve. Interrupt, MSI-X, kill and pause descriptors keep their original
 allocation paths. Each descriptor is transferred once; used eventfds are
 never recycled. Hotplug allocates normally. Remaining descriptors close with
 the reserve on delete or failed restore.
+
+## Capability and lifecycle
+
+Ping advertises `prewarm:{"version":2,"generic_prepare":true,"profile_prepare":true}`
+on supported KVM backends. One owned generic/profile state transfers to normal
+VM construction. Ordinary create discards idle preparation; receive-migration
+requires deleting it first. Invalid profile requests leave the existing reserve
+intact, while a failed resource transition drops the consumed reserve.
+
+## Template prewarm using upstream paused restore
+
+Template prewarm means a fully restored, paused VM: issue `vm.restore` with
+`memory_restore_mode:"CopyOnWrite"`, `prefault:false`, and `resume:false`, then
+issue `vm.resume` when assigned. Bind private disks, network and vsock resources
+before restore and retain them with immutable snapshot backing until teardown.
+The guest does not execute while paused; untouched RAM remains demand-paged.
+No custom template-preparation API, hotset population or KVM prefetch is added.
